          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "A migraine is a recurrent neurological disorder primarily characterized by headache but often accompanied by other symptoms like sensitivity to light, sound, and nausea. While an ordinary headache might be a general pain in the head, a migraine is typically more intense and can significantly interfere with daily activities. \n\nMigraine headaches are often one-sided, pulsating in nature, and moderate to severe in intensity. They also tend to worsen with routine physical activities and can last from several hours to three days. In contrast, ordinary headaches, such as tension-type headaches, are usually bilateral and not accompanied by the same level of debilitating symptoms.\n\nChronic migraine is defined as having headaches on fifteen or more days per month, with eight of those meeting the criteria for a migraine attack (General clinical reference on migraine).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
